{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"How do I resolve issues when I'm importing data into Amazon Personalize?","context":"### Your IAM role or S3 bucket policy doesn't have the required permissions\n\nYou get the following error message:\n\n\"Unable to Import Data into Personalize from S3Reason: Insufficient privileges for accessing data in S3.\"\n\nThis issue occurs because the IAM role that you use for Amazon Personalize doesn't have permissions to perform actions on the S3 bucket. Or, the S3 bucket policy doesn't allow the IAM service role to perform Amazon S3 actions.\n\nTo resolve this issue, [attach a policy to your IAM role that has the required permissions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/granting-personalize-s3-access.html#attaching-s3-policy-to-role). If you use batch workflows, then see [Service role policy for batch workflows](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/granting-personalize-s3-access.html#role-policy-for-batch-workflows). If you encrypted your S3 bucket with an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key, then see [Giving Amazon Personalize permission to use your AWS KMS key](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/granting-personalize-key-access.html).\n\nTo add the required permissions to your S3 bucket, see [Attaching an Amazon Personalize access policy to your S3 bucket](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/granting-personalize-s3-access.html#attach-bucket-policy). If you use batch workflows, then see [Amazon S3 bucket policy for batch workflows](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/granting-personalize-s3-access.html#bucket-policy-for-batch-workflows).\n\nIf you created the dataset in a different account from the account where the S3 bucket is located, then you must grant cross-account access. [Edit the S3 bucket policy](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies_manage-edit.html) to include the ARN of the IAM service role in the **Principal** section. An example IAM role ARN is **[redacted ARN] Personalize-user-name**.\n\nFor more information about how to configure permissions for Amazon Personalize, see [Setting up permissions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/aws-personalize-set-up-permissions.html)","url":"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/personalize-troubleshoot-import-job"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Giving Amazon Personalize access to Amazon S3 resources","context":"## Attaching an Amazon S3 policy to your Amazon Personalize service role\n\nTo attach an Amazon S3 policy to your Amazon Personalize role do the following:\n\n1. Sign in to the IAM console (<https://console.aws.amazon.com/iam/>).\n2. In the navigation pane, choose **Policies**, and choose\n **Create policy**.\n3. Choose the JSON tab, and update the policy as follows. Replace `amzn-s3-demo-bucket` with the\n name of your bucket. You can use the following policy for dataset import jobs or data deletion jobs.\n If you are using a batch workflow or creating a dataset export job, Amazon Personalize needs additional permissions. See\n Service role policy for batch workflows or\n Amazon S3 bucket policy for exporting a dataset","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/granting-personalize-s3-access.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"How do I resolve the \"Insufficient privileges for accessing data in S3\" error when I create an import job in Amazon Personalize?","context":"Resolution\n----------\n\nThis error occurs when Amazon Personalize can't read the data in the input bucket because of insufficient Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) bucket permissions.\n\nAfter you [upload all the input CSV files to your S3 bucket](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/upload-objects.html), then Amazon Personalize imports data from the input location. This means that Amazon Personalize must have [permissions to access your S3 bucket](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/granting-personalize-s3-access.html).\n\nTo grant Amazon Personalize access to your S3 bucket, complete the following steps:\n\n1. Open the [Amazon S3 console](https://s3.console.aws.amazon.com/s3/).\n2. From the list of buckets, open the bucket where you uploaded the CSV files.\n3. Choose **Permissions**, and then choose **Bucket Policy**.\n4. In the **Bucket policy editor** text box, enter the following bucket policy:\n ```plaintext\n { \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n \"Id\": \"PersonalizeS3BucketAccessPolicy\",\n \"Statement\": [\n {\n \"Sid\": \"PersonalizeS3BucketAccessPolicy\",\n \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n \"Principal\": {\n \"Service\": \"personalize.amazonaws.com\"\n },\n \"Action\": [\n \"s3:GetObject\",\n \"s3:ListBucket\"\n ],\n \"Resource\": [\n \"[redacted ARN]\",\n \"[redacted ARN]\"\n ]\n }\n ]\n }\n ```\n \n **Note:** Replace **amzn-s3-demo-bucket** with the name of your bucket.\n \n5. Choose **Save**.\n\nFor more information on setting up permissions to use Amazon Personalize, see [Setting up permissions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/aws-personalize-set-up-permissions.html).\n\nRelated information\n-------------------\n\n[Importing bulk data into Amazon Personalize with a dataset import job](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/data-prep-upload-s3.html)","url":"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/personalize-import-insufficient-privileges-s3-error"}]}}

